  "problem": "${(2x-y)}^{5}=$",
  "worked_solution": "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+\u22ef+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(2x-y)}^{5},$ we have  $a=2x,$ $b=-y,$ $n=5.$ \n\nTherefore, the first three terms of ${(2x-y)}^{5}$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x-y)^{5} & =(\\frac{5}{0})(2x)^{5}(-y)^{0}+(\\frac{5}{1})(2x)^{5-1}(-y)^{1}+(\\frac{5}{2})(2x)^{5-2}(-y)^{2}+\u22ef \\\\  & =1(2x)^{5}+5(2x)^{4}(-y)+10(2x)^{3}(-y)^{2}+\u22ef \\\\  & =32{x}^{5}-80{x}^{4}y+80{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+\u22ef.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
